A||||5||Ed||A/C Evaporator Core Freeze||||1||
Z||000000||Ed||05-25-2000||11:39 PM||||My one year old, R-134a filled, dealer repaired, A/C shows this: <P>   After about an hour of use with nice cold air coming out, the flow of air slows down.  Has ice built up around the fins of the core?   <P>Anybody experienced this or knows how to repair? The shop manual says to pressure check the refrigerant pressure and/or replace the pressure sensing switch at the accumulator.  <P>Is the switch isolated from the refrigerant by a valve which prevents release of refrigerant? Is this a do-it-yourself replacement? Parts availability?<P>Most importantly, what would be the dealer's flat rate on this if its not on warranty, or not do-it-yourself?!!        ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||05-26-2000||11:54 AM||||replacing any AC line or compressor switch requires recharging the system.<P>pressure test it first.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Denise||05-28-2000||11:15 PM||||Ed, it could be low on 134, could be a clogged orifice tube, a low pressure switch, insufficient air from the blower and even a blocked with dirt coil.  And most all require use of recovery and charging equipment. I'm in the AC biz but don't do cars. (just mine)  good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||05-29-2000||12:36 AM||||I did not have to evacuate the system to replace the pressure switch on my 86 GT.  It had a schrader (spelling) valve in the connection and all I had to do was to unscrew it quickly and put on the new one (the problem I was having was that the compressor wouldn't kick on).<P>One thing to check is if the drain hole in the bottom of the housing is plugged, not allowing the water to get away from the evaporator.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||05-29-2000||01:46 PM||||Thanks for the tips.  I've checked what I could; it goes in for repair tomorrow.  I'll post what they find. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ed||05-30-2000||10:58 PM||||It was the pressure sensor on the accumulator. <P>The air was actually too cold so that ice or frost blocked the air flow through the A/C core.  The switch cycles the A/C so this doesn't happen.  ||||reg||1
